  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued Issued by National Weather Service Glasgow MT/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nRidging aloft looks to bring general mild and dry conditions for \nmuch of the upcoming workweek; however, there will be a weak \nfrontal passage that brings a cooler low level easterly flow \ntonight into Wednesday. The effects of this will be mostly felt \nover the plains where there looks to be some nighttime and early \nmorning patchy fog development and a slight cooldown. There will \nalso be some patchy fog over some of the southwest valleys during \nthis time, especially the colder high valleys along the MT/ID/WY \nborders. Otherwise, temperatures will mostly run a few degrees \nabove average for most locations and there will be localized \nbreeziness. \n\nStrong westerly flow aloft becomes established over the Northern \nRockies during the second half of the week. The first of a series \nof fast moving zonal shortwaves will bring an initial round of \nbreezy to windy conditions Thursday into Friday. This will be \nfollowed by additional systems parading through the forecast area \nduring the weekend. Ensembles are having some difficulty resolving\nthe timing and strength of each system, but they do agree that \nthe Rocky Mountain Front and plains will be the recipients of the \nstrongest winds. With ridging generally remaining the most \ninfluential weather feature, despite the elevated westerly flow \nand shortwave passages, temperatures look to remain above average \nand precipitation will generally be spotty and confined to \nmountain areas. - RCG\n\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\n\u2026Periods of stronger westerly winds Thursday through early next \nweek\u2026\n\nEnsemble guidance is having trouble resolving the individual \nshortwaves moving through the aforementioned elevated westerly \nflow aloft. There\u2019s even some variation in precisely where the \nstrongest H500/H700 flow will be. Some of the newer deterministic \nguidance places the strongest mid-level flow north of the Canadian\nborder. With that being said, the NBM probabilistic guidance is \neyeing Friday into Saturday for the strongest winds, with \nprobabilities for 55 mph wind gusts running above 70% along the \nRocky Mountain Front and the plains west of I15. Moreover, the 60%\nchance for 75 mph wind gusts along the immediate eastern slopes \nof the Northern Rocky Mountain Front look reasonable given the \nexpectation for mountain wave activity. Of course this will be \ncontingent on the timing/phasing of strongest westerly flow aloft.\nPlains areas also look pretty windy during this time, especially \nthe central MT highway 87/200 corridor where 30 to 50 mph gusts \nwill be common. There will be additional periods of stronger winds\nlater in the weekend and into next week as the progressive \nwesterly flow continues. \n - RCG",
